WEBVTT

00:00.150 --> 00:02.520
Instructor: So, this will be a very quick video.

00:02.520 --> 00:05.760
I just wanna show that you can also configure these aliases

00:05.760 --> 00:07.890
from the command line if you prefer.

00:07.890 --> 00:10.080
It's the same exact idea

00:10.080 --> 00:12.660
of configuring any of these properties.

00:12.660 --> 00:14.070
You can do it in the file,

00:14.070 --> 00:16.110
a config file with this syntax,

00:16.110 --> 00:19.740
or you can use the dot syntax on the command line.

00:19.740 --> 00:20.820
So, it would look like this,

00:20.820 --> 00:23.133
git config --global.

00:24.180 --> 00:27.720
Then, we decide what is the alias we want to configure.

00:27.720 --> 00:30.453
Well, it's going to be alias. something.

00:31.800 --> 00:36.450
How about we do a git branch alias?

00:36.450 --> 00:38.790
Instead of making it shorter,

00:38.790 --> 00:39.900
let's do something silly.

00:39.900 --> 00:44.070
So, if you type whatismybranch,

00:44.070 --> 00:46.564
or how about showmebranches?

00:46.564 --> 00:49.050
showmebranches (laughs).

00:49.050 --> 00:51.600
Let's make that an alias for branch.

00:51.600 --> 00:55.740
So, git showmebranches should be the same as git branch.

00:55.740 --> 00:56.970
Very silly.

00:56.970 --> 00:57.803
You'll see though,

00:57.803 --> 01:00.450
if I try running git showmebranches,

01:00.450 --> 01:02.550
it actually now does auto-complete for me.

01:03.690 --> 01:05.220
It just ran git branch, right?

01:05.220 --> 01:07.680
Same output I would see if I did git branch.

01:07.680 --> 01:11.670
And if we go to this file, it shows that change.

01:11.670 --> 01:13.590
So those aliases I set up,

01:13.590 --> 01:18.570
assuming I set them up as a global alias with --global,

01:18.570 --> 01:20.820
they're added to the global config file.

01:20.820 --> 01:22.380
Otherwise, if I set them up locally,

01:22.380 --> 01:25.740
they'll be in the local config file in a specific repo.

01:25.740 --> 01:28.290
So, I probably don't want this alias,

01:28.290 --> 01:29.820
but I'll leave it for now.

01:29.820 --> 01:30.660
In the next video,

01:30.660 --> 01:32.580
I'll talk about how we can write aliases

01:32.580 --> 01:34.173
that work with arguments.
